{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "**Summary of Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was a member of the House of Saint-Germain and became Count of Tripoli following his fathers death in 1147 Raymond was the son of Raymond II and Helvisa of Ibelin he succeeded his father as count at a young age and his mother acted as regent until he came of age\n\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states during his reign he was a key figure in the conflict between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch Raymond formed an alliance with the Byzantine Empire through his marriage to Mary of Antioch the daughter of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos\n\n* **Early Life and Reign** Raymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II and Helvisa of Ibelin his father was killed in 1147 during an attack by the forces of Nur ad-Din on the castle of Tortosa Raymond IIIs mother Helvisa acted as regent for her young son until he came of age\n\nRaymond III came into conflict with King Amalric I of Jerusalem over the issue of succession and territory Raymond opposed the kings plans to annex the County of Tripoli and instead formed alliances with other nobles and the Byzantine Empire to counter the kings power\n\n* **Conflict with King Amalric I** Raymond III refused to submit to King Amalric Is authority and instead formed an alliance with the nobles of the Kingdom of Jerusalem who opposed the king Raymond also married Mary of Antioch in an effort to secure the support of the Byzantine Empire against the king\n\nRaymond III participated in several military campaigns against the Muslim forces during his reign including the Battle of Inab in 1154 where he fought alongside King Fulk of Jerusalem and the Battle of Montgisard in 1177\n\n* **Later Life and Captivity** Raymond III was captured by Saladin during the Battle of Hattin in 1187